gnment:de3c5f138eafbd29f92829e1"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 25/72","reason":"For numerators a<b and denominators p<q, the smaller pairing is a/p+b/q. Since a≥1, b≥2, p≤8, and q≤9, this is at least 1/8+2/9=25/72. The digits 1,2,8,9 attain the bound.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1994_AMC_8_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p165","cnm_p172","cnm_p080"],"id":"fraction-bounds","label":"Bound the numerators, then choose the denominators","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fraction-bounds":["math:extremal-principle","cnm_p169","cnm_p173"]},"nodeIds":["math:extremal-principle","cnm_p169","cnm_p173","cnm_p165","cnm_p172","cnm_p080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:de3c5f138eafbd29f92829e1"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (2 ordered pairs)","reason":"The equation (a-2)(b-2)=ab/2 rearranges to (a-4)(b-4)=8. With a,b>2 and b>a, the admissible factor pairs are (1,8) and (2,4), yielding (a,b)=(5,12) and (6,8).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p089","cnm_p169","cnm_096"],"id":"solution:bound-and-check-smaller-side:9fa037df25b3","label":"Bound the shorter side","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-and-check-smaller-side":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h020","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h020","cnm_042","cnm_p089","cnm_p169","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:de3c5f138eafbd29f92829e1"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 45","reason":"P(n)=[∏ from k=1 to n-1 of k/(k+1)]/(n+1)=1/[n(n+1)]. Therefore P(n)<1/2010 exactly when n(n+1)>2010. Since 44·45=1980 and 45·46=2070, the smallest n is 45.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h244","cnm_285","cnm_018","cnm_p169"],"id":"white-prefix-product","label":"Multiply the white prefix and cancel","methodDirectNodeIds":{"white-prefix-product":["cnm_h245","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h245","math:telescoping","cnm_h244","cnm_285","cnm_018","cnm_p169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:de3c5f138eafbd29f92829e1"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2","reason":"The condition (a-2)(b-2)=ab/2 rearranges to (a-4)(b-4)=8. Valid dimensions require a>4 and b>a. The positive factor pairs with first entry smaller are (1,8) and (2,4), giving (a,b)=(5,12) and (6,8).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p089","cnm_p169","cnm_145"],"id":"bound-shorter-side","label":"Restrict the shorter side first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-shorter-side":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h020","cnm_096","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h020","cnm_096","cnm_042","cnm_p089","cnm_p169","cnm_145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:de3c5f138eafbd29f92829e1"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 34/67","reason":"Let d be the distance from 100 to the nearest multiple of k. Exactly k-d residues succeed, so P(k)=1-d/k. For odd k, d≤(k-1)/2; hence k≤67 gives P(k)≥34/67. For 67≤k≤99, P(k)=2-100/k≥34/67. Equality occurs at k=67, where d=33.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_h017","cnm_p169"],"id":"balanced-remainders","label":"Count the rounding errors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"balanced-remainders":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_h020","cnm_285","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_h020","cnm_285","math:extremal-principle","math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_h017","cnm_p169"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p169"],"label":"3.NF.A.3d","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"3.NF.A.3d","description":"Compare two fractions with the same numerator or the same denominator by reasoning about their size. Recognize that comparisons are valid only when the two fractions refer to the same whole. Record the results of comparisons with the symbols >, =, or <, and justify the conclusions, e.g., by using a visual fraction model .","id":"130","label":"3.NF.A.3d","metadata":{"clusterId":"29","clusterName":"Develop Understanding Of Fractions As Numbers.","clusterOrdinal":"A","domainCode":"NF","domainId":"13","domainName":"Number And Operations-Fractions*","ordinal":"3.d","sourceCode":"3.NF.A.3.d"},"parentSourceId":"122","placement":{"grade":"3","meaning":"Native Common Core grade"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"130","status":"partial","total":44}
